WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.440 --> 00:00:07.000
This episode was made possible by generous supporters on Patreon.

00:00:07.180 --> 00:00:07.980
Hey Crazies.

00:00:07.980 --> 00:00:10.680
I recently made a video about the origin of light.

00:00:10.680 --> 00:00:15.620
I said light was an electromagnetic wave, a disturbance in the electromagnetic field.

00:00:15.620 --> 00:00:17.800
But how do we actually know light is a wave?

00:00:17.800 --> 00:00:19.120
What if we’re wrong?!

00:00:19.120 --> 00:00:22.360
(Dramatic Music)

00:00:22.360 --> 00:00:23.820
Everybody calm down!

00:00:23.820 --> 00:00:28.280
We discovered light was a wave long before we knew what type of wave it was.

00:00:28.780 --> 00:00:29.900
To the timeline!

00:00:30.080 --> 00:00:34.160
In 1690, Christiaan Huygens published his “Treatise on Light”

00:00:34.160 --> 00:00:36.060
where he suggested light was a wave.

00:00:36.060 --> 00:00:39.440
Then, in 1704, Isaac Newton published “Opticks”

00:00:39.440 --> 00:00:42.580
where he suggested light was made of tiny little objects.

00:00:42.580 --> 00:00:45.340
The debate raged on for almost a century!

00:00:45.340 --> 00:00:49.840
It wasn’t until Thomas Young performed the first double-slit experiment in 1801,

00:00:49.840 --> 00:00:54.200
that we finally proved without a doubt that light is a wave.

00:00:54.200 --> 00:00:55.510
But what about photons?

00:00:55.510 --> 00:00:56.080
Shush!

00:00:56.080 --> 00:00:59.100
So, let’s get into the details of how this double-slit thing works.

00:00:59.100 --> 00:01:02.320
First, we need some kind of wall or screen in the back.

00:01:02.320 --> 00:01:04.800
It shines wherever light hits it.

00:01:04.800 --> 00:01:07.660
Now let’s say there’s a barrier with a couple openings.

00:01:07.660 --> 00:01:11.940
If light shines on the barrier, it’ll pass some of the light and block the rest.

00:01:11.940 --> 00:01:16.260
But those openings are way too big to test anything about the light itself.

00:01:16.260 --> 00:01:18.780
For convenience, we’re using visible light

00:01:18.780 --> 00:01:23.130
and the size of these waves is on the order of 100s of nanometers.

00:01:23.130 --> 00:01:27.260
That’s tenths of thousandths of millimeters.

00:01:27.260 --> 00:01:28.300
So tiny!

00:01:28.300 --> 00:01:30.360
We don’t have to make the openings that small,

00:01:30.360 --> 00:01:34.000
but any more than 1000 times bigger and the light won’t spread out.

00:01:34.000 --> 00:01:37.880
The width and spacing needs to be at least comparable to the wavelength,

00:01:37.880 --> 00:01:39.700
which is why we call them slits.

00:01:39.700 --> 00:01:43.160
With openings that thin, something unexpected happens.

00:01:43.160 --> 00:01:45.840
Instead of two skinny lines on the back wall,

00:01:45.840 --> 00:01:49.660
we get a pattern of thick bright lines with dark spaces in-between.

00:01:49.660 --> 00:01:52.080
Something we call an interference pattern.

00:01:52.080 --> 00:01:55.840
Interference is what happens when two or more waves overlap and combine.

00:01:55.840 --> 00:01:58.540
Light is a wave in the electromagnetic field.

00:01:58.540 --> 00:01:59.780
But what about photons?

00:01:59.780 --> 00:02:00.900
I said shush!

00:02:00.900 --> 00:02:03.500
Light is a wave in the electromagnetic field,

00:02:03.500 --> 00:02:05.900
but there’s only one electromagnetic field.

00:02:05.900 --> 00:02:09.460
It can only respond to the sum of everything that’s happening to it.

00:02:09.460 --> 00:02:11.980
That’s what we call superposition.

00:02:11.980 --> 00:02:15.740
Superposition is when two or more things cause a net response

00:02:15.740 --> 00:02:20.320
that’s just the sum of the responses that would have been caused by each thing individually.

00:02:20.320 --> 00:02:22.320
It happens all the time with waves.

00:02:22.320 --> 00:02:26.760
If you’ve got two waves like this, the sum of the two looks like this.

00:02:26.760 --> 00:02:30.360
When peaks line up with peaks, we call it constructive interference

00:02:30.360 --> 00:02:34.100
because it builds up an even bigger wave, which means brighter light.

00:02:34.100 --> 00:02:37.799
When peaks line up with troughs, we call it destructive interference

00:02:37.800 --> 00:02:40.860
because the waves cancel out, which means no light.

00:02:40.860 --> 00:02:43.140
If the waves cancel, where does the energy go?

00:02:43.140 --> 00:02:45.080
It just goes somewhere else in the field.

00:02:45.080 --> 00:02:47.040
The wave doesn’t cancel everywhere!

00:02:47.040 --> 00:02:51.500
Anyway, constructive and destructive interference aren’t the only two possibilities.

00:02:51.500 --> 00:02:54.780
There are many gradual levels of brightness in-between

00:02:54.780 --> 00:02:56.900
depending on how the waves meet each other.

00:02:56.900 --> 00:03:00.940
With our double-slit, we basically have two independent sources of light.

00:03:00.940 --> 00:03:02.840
They look like this from behind.

00:03:02.840 --> 00:03:06.480
Because those slits are narrow, the light from each slit will spread out.

00:03:06.480 --> 00:03:09.240
If each slit is sending out its own light waves,

00:03:09.240 --> 00:03:12.300
those waves are going to overlap and interfere with each other.

00:03:12.300 --> 00:03:15.720
In the middle of the wall, the waves have to travel the same distance.

00:03:15.720 --> 00:03:18.820
That makes their peaks meet up and we see a bright line.

00:03:18.820 --> 00:03:20.380
We’ll label that zero.

00:03:20.380 --> 00:03:24.280
Farther along the wall, we’ll find a place where peaks meet up with troughs.

00:03:24.280 --> 00:03:25.840
That’s the first dark line.

00:03:25.840 --> 00:03:30.420
So depending on how far each of these waves has to travel compared to the other,

00:03:30.420 --> 00:03:32.320
we can get bright lines or dark lines.

00:03:32.320 --> 00:03:35.159
The next bright line is where the light from one slit

00:03:35.160 --> 00:03:38.840
has to travel one wavelength farther than the light from the other slit.

00:03:38.840 --> 00:03:42.740
We label that one because the difference in distance is one wavelength.

00:03:42.740 --> 00:03:44.999
There’s another bright line at two wavelengths

00:03:45.000 --> 00:03:46.640
and another at three wavelengths

00:03:46.640 --> 00:03:47.740
and so on.

00:03:47.740 --> 00:03:48.960
Quick side note though.

00:03:48.960 --> 00:03:52.059
You get the best results with a single color of light.

00:03:52.060 --> 00:03:55.800
The equation for where to find the lines is wavelength dependent

00:03:55.800 --> 00:03:59.060
so, if you try this with white light, the pattern is harder to make out.

00:03:59.060 --> 00:04:00.200
End of side note.

00:04:00.200 --> 00:04:01.800
How many bright lines are there?

00:04:01.800 --> 00:04:04.280
That depends on the slit width and spacing.

00:04:04.280 --> 00:04:07.839
We can only get bright lines up to 90 degrees in either direction.

00:04:07.839 --> 00:04:09.589
Eventually, we run out of room.

00:04:09.589 --> 00:04:13.120
As I mentioned before though, there aren’t just bright lines and dark lines.

00:04:13.120 --> 00:04:17.040
There’s a gradual transition in brightness from one to the other.

00:04:17.040 --> 00:04:20.280
If you graph out the predicted intensities across the wall,

00:04:20.280 --> 00:04:22.500
they match the experiment perfectly.

00:04:22.500 --> 00:04:24.080
Science at its finest!

00:04:24.080 --> 00:04:27.240
What happens if we move the wall closer or farther away?

00:04:27.240 --> 00:04:30.100
It’ll change the size of the pattern, but not its shape.

00:04:30.100 --> 00:04:35.060
That wave interference is happening all throughout the space between the slits and the wall.

00:04:35.060 --> 00:04:37.700
If you could actually see the electromagnetic field,

00:04:37.700 --> 00:04:40.960
the places you’d find dark lines would be pretty obvious.

00:04:40.960 --> 00:04:42.420
But what about photons?!

00:04:42.420 --> 00:04:44.900
Photons are waves too, OK?

00:04:44.900 --> 00:04:46.100
Seriously!

00:04:46.100 --> 00:04:49.220
Their energy is measured indirectly through their frequency.

00:04:49.220 --> 00:04:50.820
Objects don’t have frequency.

00:04:50.820 --> 00:04:51.880
Waves do.

00:04:51.880 --> 00:04:55.500
When you do the same double-slit experiment with individual photons

00:04:55.500 --> 00:04:58.580
and you’ve got a screen that can keep a record of when light hits it,

00:04:58.580 --> 00:05:01.500
the same interference pattern builds up over time.

00:05:01.500 --> 00:05:04.280
No matter how you look at it, light is a wave.

00:05:04.280 --> 00:05:07.060
We know that because it can interfere with itself

00:05:07.060 --> 00:05:10.320
and that interference produces a pattern on a screen.

00:05:10.320 --> 00:05:12.720
Got any question about diffraction or interference?

00:05:12.720 --> 00:05:14.220
Please ask in the comments.

00:05:14.220 --> 00:05:16.920
If you were hoping for more about photons, don’t worry.

00:05:16.920 --> 00:05:19.000
I’ll be going into more detail next time.

00:05:19.000 --> 00:05:20.640
Thanks for liking and sharing this video.

00:05:20.640 --> 00:05:22.880
Don’t forget to subscribe if you’d like to keep up with us.

00:05:22.880 --> 00:05:25.680
Hit that notification bell if you’re really into what we do here.

00:05:25.680 --> 00:05:29.240
And until next time, remember, it’s OK to be a little crazy.

00:05:30.220 --> 00:05:32.880
The featured comment comes from Ado Sar who asked:

00:05:32.880 --> 00:05:35.380
If wavelength can vary from medium to medium,

00:05:35.380 --> 00:05:38.360
wouldn’t it be more accurate to define color with frequency?

00:05:38.360 --> 00:05:43.520
On a scale like ours, yes, we have to define color with frequency for that exact reason.

00:05:43.520 --> 00:05:47.480
But, on a microscopic level, light is never really inside a material.

00:05:47.480 --> 00:05:49.000
Anyway, thanks for watching!

